# Explore the Logic–Physics Consistency Bridge

A physical theory can be consistent in more than one sense. Start with the accessible overview of the three levels, then open the deeper panels only when you want the details. Background reading: [Gödel's incompleteness theorems](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/goedel-incompleteness/) and [model theory](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/model-theory/).

## 1. From worlds to non-contradiction

A physical realization is a world satisfying every sentence of a theory. Under sound inference, such a witness prevents derivation of contradiction.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the physical-to-mathematical proof</summary>
Choose a realizing world. If contradiction were derivable, soundness would make contradiction true in that world. No admissible world satisfies contradiction, so no such derivation exists.
</details>

